# Shell scripts must keep LF endings.
#
# With `core.autocrlf=true` and no attribute to the contrary, git rewrites
# `*.sh` to CRLF on checkout. bash then fails on the first line that ends in
# a stray CR, so a script that passed in the working tree of the author fails
# immediately for every other contributor. `evals/skills-cli-materialization`
# hit this exactly: the grader passed on creation and broke after a
# stash/pop cycle, which is why the controls alone would not have caught it.
#
# The eval scripts under `evals/` are executed with `bash`, not sourced by a
# Windows-native runtime, so LF is the only workable ending.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Markdown, YAML and JSON stay LF as well; several are parsed by tooling that
# does not tolerate a trailing CR.
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
# TOML (mise, Worktrunk) and the TSV catalog are parsed line by line; a
# trailing CR breaks exact header checks in evals/policy-evaluation.
*.toml text eol=lf
*.tsv text eol=lf
